(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-cms-exam-4-master-exam-form-c"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 4 Master Exam — Form C","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 45-year-old man with known pulmonic regurgitation now has fatigue, ankle edema, distended neck veins and ascites. Which process explains these findings?","choices":["Right-sided heart failure","Left-sided heart failure","Pulmonary edema only","Low-output hypotension"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Retrograde flow from the pulmonary artery overloads the right ventricle, and the symptoms of pulmonic regurgitation are those of right-sided heart failure: fatigue, peripheral edema, jugular venous distension and ascites. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man has chest pain at rest with transient ST-segment elevation during the episode that resolves, and his coronary arteries show no high-grade stenosis. He smokes cigarettes and uses a recreational drug on weekends. Which substance should he be counseled to avoid because it can precipitate this condition?","choices":["Diltiazem","Nitroglycerin","Cocaine","Amlodipine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Drugs that may precipitate vasospastic angina include cocaine, ephedrine, marijuana, alcohol, amphetamine and triptans. It is relatively uncommon and often strikes those under 50. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 88, 89"},{"q":"Which test diagnoses postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome?","choices":["Stress test","Tilt-table test","Echocardiogram","Episode log"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The tilt-table test is the listed diagnostic test, with a sensitivity of about 40% and a specificity above 80%. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man is found to have a raised creatinine. What is required before chronic kidney disease can be diagnosed?","choices":["Albuminuria alone, regardless of duration","A kidney abnormality persisting three months","A single raised creatinine measurement","Structural renal disease demonstrated on imaging"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A single raised value may reflect a reversible insult, so duration is part of the definition rather than an optional confirmation. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman started a moderate-intensity statin six weeks ago for primary prevention. She feels well. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Double the intensity without labs","Repeat the lipid panel now","Recheck lipids in two years","Stop the statin, she feels well"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Adherence and percentage response are assessed with a repeat lipid panel 4 to 12 weeks after starting a statin, then every 3 to 12 months as needed; six weeks falls in that window. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman with a family history of heart failure smokes, drinks heavily and is obese. Her heart is structurally normal. Which counseling point best lowers her heart failure risk?","choices":["Wait for symptoms before changing","Quit tobacco, cut alcohol, lose weight","Family history cannot be offset at all","Start a loop diuretic as prevention"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tobacco, excessive alcohol and obesity are modifiable risk factors for heart failure. Her family history is a risk factor she cannot change, so counseling targets the ones she can. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man has yellow deposits in the creases of his palms (palmar xanthomas). His triglyceride is 410 mg/dL and total cholesterol 390 mg/dL. Genetic testing shows homozygosity for apolipoprotein E2. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Familial dysbetalipoproteinemia","Familial chylomicronemia syndrome","Metabolic syndrome","Heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An apolipoprotein E mutation with apolipoprotein E2 homozygosity lets chylomicron and very-low-density remnants accumulate, raising triglyceride and total cholesterol both to 250 to 500, with distinctive palmar xanthomas. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with two hours of chest pressure has a high-sensitivity troponin I of 16 ng/L. How should this value be interpreted?","choices":["Abnormal for a woman","Abnormal only in a man","Normal for any adult","Too low to report"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The 99th-percentile (abnormal) high-sensitivity troponin I value is 12 ng/L for women and 20 ng/L for men. At 16 ng/L, her high-sensitivity troponin I is abnormal. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman with familial chylomicronemia syndrome has had two attacks of pancreatitis. Her fasting triglyceride is 1500 mg/dL. Which management plan is most appropriate?","choices":["High-intensity statin alone","Ezetimibe and a normal diet","Apheresis every two weeks","Very low-fat diet as the mainstay"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The enzyme that clears chylomicrons is missing or ineffective in this genetic syndrome, so strict fat restriction (a very low-fat diet, under 15% of calories from fat) is the mainstay; a fibrate is the usual add-on above 500 mg/dL but works poorly here. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman has confirmed resistant hypertension: adherent, correctly measured, above goal on three complementary agents. What is the first step in management?","choices":["Refer to a hypertension specialist","Optimize the diuretic, with chlorthalidone preferred","Stop all agents and restart from the beginning","Add spironolactone immediately"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it comes before adding anything. An inadequate or inappropriate diuretic is a common reason the other two agents appear to be failing. The diuretic class is matched to renal function. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman had her diuretic dose increased 1 week ago for high readings. She now feels lightheaded whenever she stands and nearly fainted at church. Supine she is 132/78 with a heart rate of 70; standing she is 108/68 with a heart rate of 96. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Neurogenic orthostatic hypotension","Non-neurogenic orthostatic hypotension","Vasovagal reflex syncope","Chronic cardiogenic hypotension"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Medication and volume loss are typical non-neurogenic causes, and the heart rate rise from 70 to 96 shows the autonomic reflex is intact. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 7-year-old boy with homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia and under 2% of normal receptor activity asks, through his parents, why he needs frequent sessions attached to a machine. Which explanation is most appropriate?","choices":["It lowers his triglyceride level","It cures the disorder in weeks","It replaces his missing liver cells","It filters the bad cholesterol out"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Apheresis is like dialysis: the elective removal of low-density particles from the circulation, and it is the treatment of choice for the homozygous disorder. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman recovering from a myocardial infarction cannot take a beta blocker and still has exertional angina. Her echocardiogram shows preserved left ventricular function. What is accurate about adding diltiazem?","choices":["It lowers her mortality as a beta blocker would","It is preferred when ejection fraction is reduced","It relieves angina but does not lower mortality","It is avoided after every myocardial infarction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Diltiazem and verapamil do not lower mortality after infarction; when a beta blocker cannot be used they relieve angina or control rate, provided function is preserved and there is no heart failure. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 55-year-old South Asian man without diabetes has low-density lipoprotein of 150 mg/dL and a calculated 10-year atherosclerotic risk of 12%. His father had a heart attack at 50. After discussion he favors treatment. What is the most appropriate therapy?","choices":["Lifestyle alone without any drug","High-intensity statin with ezetimibe","A fibrate for his cholesterol","Moderate-intensity statin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. At intermediate risk with risk enhancers (South Asian ancestry, premature family history) and a discussion that favors therapy, start a moderate-intensity statin to lower low-density lipoprotein by 30% or more. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"ST elevation in II, III and aVF most often points to which culprit artery?","choices":["Left marginal branch","Diagonal branch","Left anterior descending artery","Right coronary artery"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The inferior leads II, III and aVF point to the right coronary artery (and/or the left circumflex). Inferior equals right coronary artery is the pairing to remember. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 61, 62"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman standing at an outdoor event in the heat develops tunnel vision, lightheadedness and dizziness but is still conscious. If her pressure keeps falling, what is likely to follow?","choices":["A prolonged coma","Supine hypertension","A generalized seizure","A brief faint"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her symptoms are near-syncope, which precedes syncope: a transient, self-limited loss of consciousness followed by spontaneous recovery. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which causes fall under ischemic cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Diabetes, thyroid disease and lupus","Congenital structural heart disease","Infarction, ongoing ischemia or shock","Alcohol and chemotherapy exposure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ischemic cardiomyopathy covers myocardial infarction, persistent or chronic intermittent myocardial ischemia, and hypoperfusion from shock. Everything else is a non-ischemic cause. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"How is familial hypercholesterolemia inherited?","choices":["Codominant genetic disorder","Acquired, not inherited","X-linked recessive disorder","Mitochondrial inheritance"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is codominant: homozygous patients carry two mutant alleles and heterozygous patients one, and the homozygous form presents in childhood while the heterozygous form is often found on screening. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which symptom may be the only sign of silent ischemia?","choices":["Shortness of breath","Fever with chills","Crushing chest pressure","Tearing back pain"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Silent ischemia presents without chest pain, with shortness of breath, cough, nausea and vomiting, abdominal pain, diaphoresis, dizziness or fatigue instead. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man with hypertriglyceridemia has a total cholesterol of 250 mg/dL, high-density lipoprotein of 40 mg/dL and triglyceride of 300 mg/dL. What is his non-high-density cholesterol?","choices":["190 mg/dL","290 mg/dL","210 mg/dL","50 mg/dL"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Non-high-density cholesterol is total cholesterol minus high-density cholesterol: 250 minus 40 is 210 mg/dL. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman has fatigue and breathlessness with exertion. Echocardiogram shows an ejection fraction of 45%, and her fraction has never been lower. Which heart failure group describes her?","choices":["Mildly reduced ejection fraction","Improved ejection fraction","Reduced ejection fraction","Preserved ejection fraction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. 45% falls in the 41 to 49% band, and with no earlier value of 40% or less there is nothing to make it improved ejection fraction. Her treatment then resembles preserved ejection fraction. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 39-year-old woman is referred with hypertension diagnosed at 32 and an adrenal nodule found incidentally on imaging. What is the underlying abnormality if aldosteronism is confirmed?","choices":["Autonomous aldosterone production independent of renin","Glucocorticoid excess promoting sodium retention","Catecholamine excess from an adrenal medullary tumor","Excess renin driving aldosterone production"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The adrenal gland secretes aldosterone without the usual renin-angiotensin signal, so sodium is retained and potassium wasted regardless of volume status. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man who injects drugs has fevers for two weeks. There is a holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border near the epigastrium that grows louder with inspiration (Carvallo sign), and his neck veins pulsate. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Tricuspid stenosis","Pulmonic stenosis","Tricuspid regurgitation","Mitral regurgitation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border near the epigastrium that increases with inspiration is tricuspid regurgitation; in an injection drug user the primary cause is infective endocarditis. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 71, 70"},{"q":"What triggers situational reflex syncope?","choices":["A rapid ventricular rhythm","Standing after autonomic nerve damage","Certain bodily functions or actions","Emotional stress, fear or pain"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Situational syncope is triggered by certain bodily functions or physical actions, such as urination at night, coughing or sneezing. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has combined right and left heart failure with symptoms at rest despite optimized guideline-directed therapy. Which course is most appropriate to discuss?","choices":["Continuing dobutamine at home","Transplant evaluation or comfort care","Adding a calcium channel blocker","Stopping all medicines and observing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Combined right and left failure is managed with symptom control, comfort care and heart transplant. When optimized therapy no longer controls symptoms, those are the options to raise. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction is comfortable at rest but becomes breathless after only a few steps across a room. Which New York Heart Association class describes him?","choices":["Class IV","Class III","Class I","Class II"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms with only slight exertion, such as a few steps across a room, and none at rest are class III. Class II is symptoms with ordinary exertion and class IV is symptoms at rest. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes and hypertension has no cardiac symptoms. Her echocardiogram is normal. Which drug class is recommended first to lower her risk of heart failure?","choices":["Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonist","Beta blocker as the first agent","Loop diuretic such as bumetanide","Sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Risk factors with a normal heart and no symptoms are stage A, at risk for heart failure, and the one strong drug recommendation there is a sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor in patients with diabetes, not a beta blocker. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man without cardiovascular disease has hypertension and smokes. His clinician wants to estimate his 10-year risk of atherosclerotic disease to guide statin therapy. Which tool is appropriate?","choices":["Skin biopsy for receptors","Pooled Cohort Equation","Metabolic syndrome criteria","Fundoscopic examination"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It estimates 10-year primary risk of atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease in patients 40 to 79 without pre-existing disease, using variables such as age, sex, cholesterol, systolic pressure, diabetes and smoking. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with fatigue, cold intolerance and constipation is found to have blood pressure of 146/96 with a predominantly diastolic elevation. Which mechanism fits?","choices":["Hyperthyroidism raising cardiac output","Hypothyroidism raising vascular resistance","Autonomous aldosterone secretion","Catecholamine excess from an adrenal tumor"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hypothyroidism raises systemic vascular resistance, which lifts the diastolic pressure, whereas hyperthyroidism raises cardiac output and lifts the systolic. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man was treated in the intensive care unit with dobutamine for acute heart failure. His daughter asks whether he can continue it at home to keep his heart strong. What is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Only if his blood pressure is high","Yes, in place of his other medicines","Inotropes are for intensive care only","Yes, as long-term home maintenance"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Inotropic therapy, including dobutamine, norepinephrine, epinephrine and milrinone, is for intensive care use only. Outpatient therapy is guideline-directed therapy for his ejection fraction group. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 42-year-old woman has tricuspid stenosis seen on an echocardiogram and mild leg swelling. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Cardiology referral","No follow-up needed","Balloon valvotomy now","Mechanical replacement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tricuspid stenosis is referred when it is seen on an echocardiogram or when there are heart failure related symptoms, both of which she has. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"What treatment does mild to moderate pulmonic stenosis need?","choices":["None","A loop diuretic","Balloon valvuloplasty","Valve replacement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mild to moderate pulmonic stenosis needs no treatment and carries a 94% 20-year survival. Severe symptomatic disease is treated with diuretics and pulmonic balloon valvuloplasty. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Which set of findings reflects inadequate tissue perfusion in heart failure?","choices":["Tachycardia, a third heart sound and neck veins","Hepatomegaly, nausea and ascites","Fatigue, near-syncope and obtundation","Orthopnea, crackles and edema"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Inadequate tissue perfusion shows as weakness, fatigue, lethargy, malaise, syncope or near-syncope and obtundation, alongside renal dysfunction. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which description fits the aortic stenosis murmur?","choices":["Blowing holosystolic, to the axilla","Blowing early diastolic, to the apex","Low diastolic rumble after a snap","Harsh systolic ejection, to the carotids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Aortic stenosis gives a harsh, systolic, crescendo-decrescendo ejection murmur at the second right intercostal space (right upper sternal border) that radiates to the carotids. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 79-year-old man with Parkinson disease faints when he gets up at night. His readings show a postural fall with a minimal heart rate rise. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["Cut fluids to avoid night trips","Stop all activity for a month","Leap up to trigger his reflexes","Sit first, then stand up slowly"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Slow positional changes are listed treatment for orthostatic syncope, and understanding why helps him follow the advice. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman with very heavy menstrual bleeding has fatigue, palpitations, ankle edema and a raised B-type natriuretic peptide. Echocardiogram is being arranged. Which additional test is most likely to identify the driver of her heart failure?","choices":["Complete blood count","Chest radiograph","Right heart catheterization","Cardiac troponin level"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Anemia is one of the causes of high-output failure, and the complete blood count is part of the high-output workup with the echocardiogram, metabolic panel and natriuretic peptide. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man arrives at the emergency department with 30 minutes of chest pressure. Which pair of studies does every patient with chest pain receive?","choices":["Calcium score and a lipid panel","Coronary angiography and echocardiogram","Treadmill stress test and D-dimer","Electrocardiogram and chest radiograph"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. All chest pain patients get a chest radiograph and a stat electrocardiogram. Blood work (complete blood count, metabolic panel, coagulation studies, troponin) follows. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with aortic insufficiency has an echocardiogram showing a regurgitant fraction of 62%. Which severity does this indicate?","choices":["Mild regurgitation","Moderate regurgitation","Severe regurgitation","Ungradeable by echocardiogram"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Aortic regurgitation is severe when 50% or more of the blood that passed the valve comes back. A fraction of 30 to 49% is moderate and below 30% is mild. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which warning features suggest renovascular disease?","choices":["Weak or delayed femoral pulses","Proximal muscle weakness and broad purple striae","An abdominal bruit with asymmetric kidneys","Episodic headache, sweating and palpitations"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Flash pulmonary edema is the one most easily missed, and it is also one of the features that most strongly argues for revascularization rather than medical management alone. Resistant or abrupt elevation and recurrent flash pulmonary edema are the other warning features. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man has a faint while urinating at night, with nausea and sweating beforehand and quick recovery. Which test is used to exclude a cardiac dysrhythmia?","choices":["A tilt-table test","A blood glucose level","Orthostatic vital signs","An electrocardiogram"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A normal electrocardiogram lowers suspicion of a dysrhythmia, just as a normal glucose excludes hypoglycemia and blood tests address infection. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 54-year-old arrives late for a routine visit, is taken straight to the room and has her blood pressure measured immediately while she describes her morning. The cuff is placed over her sleeve and her legs are crossed. The reading is 152/94. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Reassure her that a single elevated reading is meaningless","Diagnose stage 2 hypertension and start two agents","Order plasma metanephrines to exclude pheochromocytoma","Repeat it after five minutes of quiet rest"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Every departure here inflates the reading: no rest period, talking during the measurement, a sleeved arm and crossed legs. A diagnosis rests on readings taken to standard, and acting on this one would start treatment on an artifact. Correct technique matters as much as the rest period. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with a mechanical mitral valve asks whether she could take daily aspirin instead of her current anticoagulant. Which long-term therapy is recommended for her?","choices":["A vitamin K antagonist","Aspirin in place of it","No drug after one year","Heparin every day"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With a mechanical heart valve, vitamin K antagonist therapy is recommended over no vitamin K antagonist and over antiplatelet agents. Warfarin is the one most commonly used. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman wore a defibrillator vest for three months after an infarction, with optimized guideline-directed therapy and cardiac rehabilitation. Her repeat echocardiogram shows an ejection fraction of 28%. She is class II and expected to live many years. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Continue the vest indefinitely","Implantable cardioverter-defibrillator","Stop therapy and recheck in a year","Heart transplant evaluation now"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The pathway ends with an implantable device when the repeat echocardiogram shows no improvement. She also meets the criteria: ejection fraction 35% or less, class II or III, optimal therapy and life expectancy of at least one year. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 73-year-old woman with neurogenic orthostatic hypotension from diabetes also has chronic kidney disease. She remains symptomatic despite slow position changes. Which treatment should be avoided?","choices":["Midodrine","Droxidopa","Slow position changes","Fludrocortisone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Chronic kidney disease is one of the listed settings, with supine hypertension and severe heart disease, in which midodrine is avoided. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man is newly diagnosed with heart failure and will start a loop diuretic. Which laboratory panel should be checked before starting?","choices":["Coagulation panel","Complete metabolic panel","Hepatic function panel","Thyroid function panel"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The metabolic panel gives potassium and renal function (blood urea nitrogen, creatinine and filtration rate), both of which a potassium-wasting loop diuretic affects. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman with mitral valve prolapse has now developed symptomatic severe mitral regurgitation with a falling ejection fraction. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["A mitral clip or valve replacement","Aortic valve replacement","Percutaneous balloon valvotomy","Reassurance with no treatment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Prolapse usually needs no treatment, but if it progresses to mitral regurgitation the options are a clip, transcatheter mitral valve replacement, or biological or mechanical replacement. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 54, 52"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman who smokes a pack of cigarettes a day asks whether smoking really matters for her heart at her age. Which counseling point is accurate?","choices":["It matters only after menopause","It raises risk only in men","It is a strong predictor at her age","It matters only with diabetes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Smoking is a modifiable coronary risk factor and a strong predictor, especially in women younger than 45. Her age makes the counseling more urgent, not less. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man reports lightheadedness and dimming vision within a minute or two of standing, which settles when he sits. His examination at rest is normal. Which test should be performed first?","choices":["Orthostatic vital signs","An echocardiogram","A tilt-table test","A stress test"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pressure and heart rate taken supine and again within 3 minutes of standing show whether orthostatic criteria are met and which type the heart rate response suggests. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which are the earliest measurable markers of hypertensive kidney damage?","choices":["Hematuria and casts on urinalysis","Rising albuminuria and falling filtration rate","A rising serum potassium concentration","A further rise in the blood pressure itself"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and both are watched as trends rather than single values, because it is the direction of travel over time that identifies progression early enough to act on. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman is newly diagnosed with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (fraction 28%). She is breathless with ordinary exertion but comfortable at rest, and takes no renin-angiotensin drug. Which agent fits her symptom class?","choices":["Dobutamine","Metolazone alone","Sacubitril-valsartan","Diltiazem"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms with exertion are New York Heart Association class II, and the angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor is recommended for class II to III in reduced ejection fraction. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man is admitted with an inferior ST-elevation myocardial infarction that extends into the right ventricle, with ST elevation in the right-sided lead V4R. His daughter asks why he is not being given nitroglycerin for his chest pain. What should she be told?","choices":["It would slow his heart rate","It would lower his preload","It would cause bleeding","It would cause coronary spasm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In a right ventricular infarct, nitroglycerin is not given: decreasing the preload reduces the Frank-Starling effect on the right ventricle, which can fail into right-sided heart failure, dropping output and precipitating shock. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 62, 63"},{"q":"Which lipoprotein consists mostly of cholesterol and supplies cholesterol for cellular needs?","choices":["Low-density lipoprotein","Chylomicrons","Very-low-density lipoprotein","High-density lipoprotein"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Low-density lipoprotein consists mostly of cholesterol, is used to provide cholesterol for cellular needs, and its increased levels put patients at risk for atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man has a holosystolic blowing murmur at the apex radiating to the axilla. He is asked to squeeze a hand grip firmly for a minute while the examiner listens. How does the murmur change?","choices":["It becomes diastolic","It does not change","It becomes louder","It becomes softer"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Squatting and handgrip increase the mitral regurgitation murmur: peripheral vascular resistance to ventricular ejection rises and pushes more regurgitation back into the left atrium. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 64-year-old hypertensive man also has a raised low-density lipoprotein. What principle should guide his management?","choices":["Treat the lipids first and defer blood pressure control","Treat total cardiovascular risk, not pressure alone","Lipid therapy is unnecessary once blood pressure reaches goal","Treat the blood pressure and address lipids only if it fails"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hypertension accelerates atherosclerosis through shear stress, intimal injury, lipid oxidation and smooth muscle proliferation, so antiplatelet and lipid-lowering therapy are considered where indicated. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with a prior heart attack and reduced ejection fraction collapsed abruptly while walking, with no warning except a brief racing heartbeat. He is pale, his pressure is 86/52 and his heart rate is 150 and regular. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Vasovagal reflex syncope","Non-neurogenic orthostatic hypotension","Situational reflex syncope","Cardiogenic syncope from an arrhythmia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Abrupt collapse with minimal prodrome after palpitations, in a patient with coronary disease and reduced ejection fraction, with a rapid regular rhythm, points to an arrhythmic cardiac cause. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man admitted with acute heart failure has diuresed well. On the day of planned discharge his B-type natriuretic peptide is 360. What does this predischarge level suggest?","choices":["Acute heart failure is still likely","Higher risk of 30-day readmission","Lower risk of 30-day readmission","The level carries no prognostic value"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A predischarge level below 430 carries a lower risk of readmission within 30 days, which supports discharge planning. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with a myocardial infarction three years ago has breathlessness on exertion and orthopnea (breathlessness lying flat). Blood pressure is 104/68. There is a third heart sound (an extra sound in early diastole just after the second heart sound) and bibasilar crackles. B-type natriuretic peptide is 820. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Diastolic failure from hypertensive heart disease","Isolated right-sided failure from the lungs","Systolic failure from ischemic cardiomyopathy","High-output failure from increased demand"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Every link of the systolic chain is here: ischemic cardiomyopathy after infarction, a low-normal blood pressure, a third heart sound from rapid filling of a dilated ventricle, and pulmonary edema, confirmed by an elevated natriuretic peptide. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman with a hypertensive emergency has her pressure reduced from 240/130 to 125/75 within thirty minutes. She then develops a new hemiparesis. What happened?","choices":["Hyperkalemia precipitated the event","She developed rebound hypertension","The reduction was appropriate and the deficit is unrelated","Autoregulation had adapted to the higher pressure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her organs had reset the range over which they protect their own perfusion, so a value that would be safe in anyone else left her brain underperfused. Reducing it that fast left her brain underperfused. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman has an hour of substernal pressure. Her electrocardiogram shows ST-segment depression of 2 mm in V5 and V6 without ST elevation, and her standard troponin I is 0.62 ng/mL. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Infarction with ST elevation","Vasospastic angina","Unstable angina","Infarction without ST elevation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. ST-segment depression above 1 mm and/or T-wave inversion in two or more consecutive leads, with symptoms and an elevated troponin I (standard troponin I above 0.04 ng/mL), is a non-ST-elevation infarction. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 47, 86; Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"A 39-year-old woman who emigrated from a country where rheumatic fever is common has breathlessness on exertion and orthopnea (breathlessness lying flat). A diastolic rumble is heard at the apex. Which is the most appropriate initial test?","choices":["Electrocardiogram only","No test until symptoms worsen","Cardiac catheterization","Transthoracic echocardiogram"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Any patient with symptoms suggestive of mitral stenosis, such as dyspnea on exertion or orthopnea, should get an echocardiogram. It shows a large left atrium and the stenotic mitral valve. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 43, 39"}]};
